WEBVTT

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:13.192
In 1872, the French writer Jules Verne published a famous novel: Around the World in Eighty

00:00:13.192 --> 00:00:14.645
Days.

00:00:14.655 --> 00:00:19.884
The hero is Phileas Fogg, a gentleman in London who lives by the clock.

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:25.016
Fogg bets half his money that he can travel around the whole world in eighty days.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:31.550
Tonight — Wednesday, the second of October, 1872 — at a quarter to nine, his train leaves

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.976
London.

00:00:32.984 --> 00:00:36.305
We follow his route, starting today.

00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:39.437
Look at the map: only London is lit.

00:00:39.445 --> 00:00:42.582
Day by day, we light the rest.

00:00:42.589 --> 00:00:47.555
One more name: Passepartout, Fogg's new French valet.

00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:50.082
His watch is four minutes slow.

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:52.121
Remember the watch.

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:55.924
First stop: Fogg's street, Savile Row.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.322
This is Savile Row, a quiet street in Mayfair.

00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:04.522
The novel starts here.

00:01:04.529 --> 00:01:09.390
Phileas Fogg lives on this street — at No. 7, the book says.

00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:13.153
Tonight he will leave from that door to go round the world.

00:01:13.161 --> 00:01:19.048
Look in the shop windows: rolls of cloth, chalk marks, half-made jackets.

00:01:19.056 --> 00:01:23.298
In 1872 this was already a street of tailors.

00:01:23.306 --> 00:01:25.087
It still is.

00:01:25.095 --> 00:01:30.008
The tailor Henry Poole opened his door on the Row in 1846.

00:01:30.016 --> 00:01:35.008
The shop is still here — at No. 15 since 1982.

00:01:35.016 --> 00:01:40.187
The firm says that in 1865 they made a short evening jacket for the Prince of Wales —

00:01:40.187 --> 00:01:44.022
the grandfather, they say, of the dinner jacket.

00:01:44.029 --> 00:01:50.424
One more stop: on the roof of No. 3, in January 1969, the Beatles played their famous

00:01:50.424 --> 00:01:52.429
rooftop concert.

00:01:52.437 --> 00:01:55.798
And one small secret about No. 7.

00:01:55.806 --> 00:02:00.482
Verne wrote that the playwright Sheridan died there in 1814.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.772
In fact Sheridan lived at No. 14, and died in 1816.

00:02:05.779 --> 00:02:08.758
No. 14 has a plaque for him.

00:02:08.766 --> 00:02:12.377
Fogg has no plaque — he lived only in the book.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.117
Fogg's day, hour by hour.

00:02:18.128 --> 00:02:20.186
He gets up at eight.

00:02:20.194 --> 00:02:23.844
Tea and toast at twenty-three minutes past eight.

00:02:23.852 --> 00:02:30.357
Shaving water at nine thirty-seven, at exactly thirty degrees — eighty-six Fahrenheit.

00:02:30.365 --> 00:02:34.225
He leaves at half past eleven; home at midnight.

00:02:34.233 --> 00:02:40.659
This morning, at twenty-nine minutes past eleven, Passepartout walked into this house.

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.436
A Parisian, about thirty.

00:02:43.444 --> 00:02:49.370
He was a singer, a circus rider, a gymnastics teacher, a fireman in Paris.

00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:52.738
Then ten English houses in five years.

00:02:52.746 --> 00:02:55.357
He wants a quiet life now.

00:02:55.365 --> 00:02:59.120
We like him already — the man with the slow watch.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.916
Half past eleven.

00:03:03.927 --> 00:03:07.287
Fogg leaves the house and walks to his club.

00:03:07.295 --> 00:03:12.643
He counts his steps as he goes: one thousand one hundred and fifty-one of them.

00:03:12.651 --> 00:03:18.243
His way runs down Savile Row, right into Burlington Gardens, then left down Old Bond Street

00:03:18.243 --> 00:03:19.840
to Piccadilly.

00:03:19.848 --> 00:03:24.419
Across the road, and down the long slope of St James's Street.

00:03:24.427 --> 00:03:28.103
At the bottom he turns east, into Pall Mall.

00:03:28.111 --> 00:03:32.927
From his front door to the club door is one thousand one hundred and twenty metres: about

00:03:32.927 --> 00:03:34.735
one kilometre.

00:03:34.743 --> 00:03:38.603
So each step is about ninety-seven centimetres.

00:03:38.611 --> 00:03:40.735
He never hurries.

00:03:40.743 --> 00:03:44.011
And this is the street he is walking into.

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:49.498
Pall Mall had gas lamps in 1807, one of the first streets in London.

00:03:49.506 --> 00:03:54.564
Sixty-five years before Fogg, the lamps here were already the future.

00:03:54.572 --> 00:03:59.064
This is clubland: a row of private clubs on the south side.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:01.485
Three of them stand together.

00:04:01.493 --> 00:04:07.085
In front of us, the grand stone front with the tall windows: 104 Pall Mall, the Reform

00:04:07.085 --> 00:04:11.380
Club, by Charles Barry, opened 1841.

00:04:11.387 --> 00:04:13.629
That one is Fogg's.

00:04:13.637 --> 00:04:19.703
A little to the left, No. 106, the Travellers, Barry's first Italian palazzo, finished

00:04:19.703 --> 00:04:21.998
in 1832.

00:04:22.006 --> 00:04:26.222
And beyond it, No. 107, the Athenaeum.

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:29.248
One turn of the head covers all three.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.603
This is the door of Fogg's club: the Reform Club.

00:04:35.613 --> 00:04:41.895
It is real, and still here — 104 Pall Mall, since 1836.

00:04:41.903 --> 00:04:47.921
Its founders had backed the Reform Act of 1832 — that is where the name comes from.

00:04:47.929 --> 00:04:54.842
In the book, Fogg spends every day here until midnight: meals, newspapers, cards.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:59.579
The architect Charles Barry finished the building in 1841.

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:05.789
Stone outside; inside, a grand hall two floors high, under a glass roof.

00:05:05.797 --> 00:05:08.355
Look up at the tall windows.

00:05:08.363 --> 00:05:14.303
Here Fogg ate his breakfast: grilled fish with Reading sauce — remember that sauce.

00:05:14.311 --> 00:05:19.645
Roast beef, a fruit tart, cheese, and the club's famous tea.

00:05:19.653 --> 00:05:24.363
Now three pictures: the front today, the architect's plan of the front, and the great

00:05:24.363 --> 00:05:27.474
hall, drawn in 1841.

00:05:27.482 --> 00:05:30.566
No camera has been inside for us.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:37.225
Verne describes the inside of the club with much detail.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.412
Mosaic floors.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:44.162
A gallery under a dome, held up by twenty red stone columns.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.570
Blue painted windows.

00:05:46.578 --> 00:05:50.280
A dining room with nine windows onto a garden.

00:05:50.288 --> 00:05:53.294
Compare that with Barry's real building.

00:05:53.302 --> 00:05:56.070
Which of these details is real?

00:05:56.078 --> 00:05:59.728
A dining-room with nine windows onto the garden.

00:05:59.736 --> 00:06:01.373
Exact.

00:06:01.381 --> 00:06:07.057
Nine tall casement windows in the coffee-room's south wall, and the garden is still there.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.205
Evening, at the club.

00:06:12.216 --> 00:06:17.282
Fogg plays whist with five fellow members — four hands at the table, the same five men

00:06:17.282 --> 00:06:18.892
every night.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:23.650
In the story — only in the story — a thief has stolen fifty-five thousand pounds from

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.589
the Bank of England.

00:06:25.597 --> 00:06:28.313
Where could such a man hide now?

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:33.650
So a newspaper, the Daily Telegraph, counts the days: you can now go around the world

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:35.484
in eighty days.

00:06:35.492 --> 00:06:40.024
At the table this becomes a wager — a bet, with money on it.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:45.405
Andrew Stuart, an engineer, bets four thousand pounds that it cannot be done.

00:06:45.413 --> 00:06:50.274
Fogg bets twenty thousand pounds — half his money — that it can.

00:06:50.282 --> 00:06:53.221
Look at the card: six names.

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:56.129
Stuart first, Fogg last.

00:06:56.137 --> 00:07:01.176
Fogg must be back in this same room on Saturday, the twenty-first of December, at a quarter

00:07:01.176 --> 00:07:03.155
to nine at night.

00:07:03.163 --> 00:07:05.589
Exactly eighty days.

00:07:05.597 --> 00:07:07.958
It really was a Saturday.

00:07:07.966 --> 00:07:11.840
Fogg's motto: 'The unforeseen does not exist.'

00:07:11.847 --> 00:07:16.576
Fogg's bank, Baring's, failed in 1995.

00:07:16.584 --> 00:07:19.234
The wager has outlived the bank.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.681
How could anyone believe this plan?

00:07:24.692 --> 00:07:27.763
Because the world had just become smaller.

00:07:27.771 --> 00:07:32.336
When one of the card players said the world was big, Fogg answered with three words: '

00:07:32.336 --> 00:07:34.105
It was once.'

00:07:34.113 --> 00:07:36.434
Look at the three lights.

00:07:36.442 --> 00:07:40.657
The Suez Canal opened in November 1869.

00:07:40.665 --> 00:07:46.434
The American railroad, coast to coast, was joined in May 1869.

00:07:46.442 --> 00:07:51.526
The last rail link across India closed the gap in March 1870.

00:07:51.534 --> 00:07:57.105
Three doors, all opened within ten months, two years before tonight.

00:07:57.113 --> 00:07:59.460
Now watch the line draw.

00:07:59.468 --> 00:08:02.578
London to Suez: seven days.

00:08:02.586 --> 00:08:06.368
Suez to Bombay, by ship: thirteen.

00:08:06.376 --> 00:08:10.157
Bombay to Calcutta, by rail: three.

00:08:10.165 --> 00:08:13.302
Calcutta to Hong Kong: thirteen.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:16.420
Hong Kong to Yokohama: six.

00:08:16.428 --> 00:08:20.407
Yokohama to San Francisco: twenty-two.

00:08:20.415 --> 00:08:24.776
San Francisco to New York, by rail: seven.

00:08:24.784 --> 00:08:27.802
New York back to London: nine.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.973
Add them up: eighty.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.046
Twenty-five past seven.

00:08:35.056 --> 00:08:37.654
Fogg comes home from the club.

00:08:37.662 --> 00:08:43.404
He calls Passepartout and says: 'We start for Dover and Calais in ten minutes.'

00:08:43.412 --> 00:08:48.443
Dover is on the English coast; Calais is across the sea, in France.

00:08:48.451 --> 00:08:54.509
Where to? 'We are going round the world.' 'In eighty days.'

00:08:54.517 --> 00:08:58.680
Passepartout, remember, wanted a quiet life.

00:08:58.688 --> 00:09:00.812
Now help him pack.

00:09:00.819 --> 00:09:05.417
One carpet-bag — one travel bag — must hold everything.

00:09:05.425 --> 00:09:08.983
Two shirts and three pairs of stockings each.

00:09:08.991 --> 00:09:11.904
A mackintosh and a travelling-cloak.

00:09:11.912 --> 00:09:17.219
The red-bound Bradshaw — a thick book of train times, hundreds of pages.

00:09:17.227 --> 00:09:22.088
And a fat roll of Bank of England notes: twenty thousand pounds.

00:09:22.096 --> 00:09:25.062
No books — the house has none.

00:09:25.069 --> 00:09:28.048
The electric clock stays on the mantel.

00:09:28.056 --> 00:09:32.338
And so, tonight, does the gas — remember that.

00:09:32.346 --> 00:09:37.088
Two shirts, three pairs of stockings, and half a fortune.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:44.792
Fogg and Passepartout take a cab at the end of Savile Row — probably a hansom, a fast

00:09:44.792 --> 00:09:46.942
two-wheeled horse cab.

00:09:46.953 --> 00:09:51.708
To the driver, Fogg is a 'swell': a gentleman in good clothes.

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:55.734
The fare is sixpence a mile — or part of a mile.

00:09:55.742 --> 00:10:01.353
Charing Cross is about a mile and a half away, so call it one shilling: a 'bob'.

00:10:01.361 --> 00:10:06.432
Watch the line on the 1872 map, south and east to the Strand.

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:12.405
London already had an Underground railway — nine years old, full of steam and smoke.

00:10:12.413 --> 00:10:14.826
Fogg takes a cab instead.

00:10:14.834 --> 00:10:21.221
Listen: hooves on stone, and fast French dance music for Passepartout, the Parisian.

00:10:21.229 --> 00:10:24.747
They left the club at twenty-five past seven.

00:10:24.755 --> 00:10:28.208
They reach Charing Cross at twenty past eight.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:34.944
This is the Strand, and ahead is Charing Cross station.

00:10:34.954 --> 00:10:39.288
Forget 'old London': for Fogg, this was new London.

00:10:39.296 --> 00:10:44.512
The station opened in January 1864 — eight years old that night.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.946
The hotel behind the stone cross: 1865.

00:10:48.954 --> 00:10:52.473
The cross itself: 1865.

00:10:52.481 --> 00:10:55.959
The Underground railway: nine years old.

00:10:55.967 --> 00:10:59.762
The Embankment along the river: two years old.

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:04.078
And the clock tower we call Big Ben: thirteen years old.

00:11:04.086 --> 00:11:08.104
Much of what Fogg passes tonight is younger than his valet.

00:11:08.112 --> 00:11:15.262
One thing you cannot see: the station's huge iron roof, 155 metres in one span.

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:18.815
It fell in 1905 and was replaced.

00:11:18.823 --> 00:11:21.091
That is all we will say.

00:11:21.099 --> 00:11:24.052
Then the one warm moment of the evening.

00:11:24.059 --> 00:11:27.183
A barefoot woman with a child asks for money.

00:11:27.191 --> 00:11:31.341
Fogg gives her the twenty guineas he has just won at cards.

00:11:31.349 --> 00:11:34.038
He does not even stop walking.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:42.902
On the left: a drawing from 1872 — the hotel and forecourt as Fogg saw them.

00:11:42.913 --> 00:11:45.799
On the right: the cross today.

00:11:45.807 --> 00:11:50.681
Six platforms behind it, and still about one train an hour to Dover.

00:11:50.689 --> 00:11:54.549
The hotel is still a hotel, under a new name.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.909
Stop here.

00:11:58.919 --> 00:12:01.069
Two things to find.

00:12:01.077 --> 00:12:06.209
The first is straight above you: follow the cross up to its top — twenty-one metres, seventy

00:12:06.209 --> 00:12:08.333
feet, of stone.

00:12:08.341 --> 00:12:14.148
It is a copy, put up in 1865 — the year the hotel behind it opened.

00:12:14.156 --> 00:12:16.306
The second is harder.

00:12:16.314 --> 00:12:20.933
Turn to your right, out of the station yard, and look about two hundred metres towards

00:12:20.933 --> 00:12:22.912
Trafalgar Square.

00:12:22.919 --> 00:12:28.530
You cannot quite see him from here, but a statue of King Charles the First rides there.

00:12:28.538 --> 00:12:33.319
The first cross, from 1291, stood on that spot.

00:12:33.327 --> 00:12:39.004
London still measures its distances from that statue, not from the stone above your head.

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:44.823
Twenty to nine, still under the roof.

00:12:44.833 --> 00:12:48.102
Look up — what is the weather doing tonight?

00:12:48.109 --> 00:12:51.523
A dark night and a fine, steady rain.

00:12:51.531 --> 00:12:53.786
Verne's own words.

00:12:53.794 --> 00:12:57.944
Rain on the station roof, then rain on the carriage window.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.717
It's twenty to nine, and it's raining.

00:13:03.727 --> 00:13:09.338
That's Passepartout, with the bag — he's known his master for about nine hours.

00:13:09.346 --> 00:13:11.575
Who is your new master?

00:13:12.295 --> 00:13:17.347
<v Passepartout>Mr Phileas Fogg of Savile Row, monsieur — the most exact and settled gentleman in the

00:13:17.347 --> 00:13:20.168
<v Passepartout>United Kingdom, they told me.

00:13:20.179 --> 00:13:27.079
<v Passepartout>Rises at eight, tea and toast at twenty-three past, out at half past eleven, home at midnight.

00:13:27.087 --> 00:13:28.960
<v Passepartout>A real machine.

00:13:28.968 --> 00:13:31.803
<v Passepartout>I do not mind serving a machine.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:35.526
<v Passepartout>I have known him since twenty-nine minutes past eleven.

00:13:36.268 --> 00:13:38.615
We've got a train to catch.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:46.542
This postcard is from 1905, thirty-three years after Fogg.

00:13:46.552 --> 00:13:52.294
The same platforms — and the old roof, probably in its last months before it fell.

00:13:52.302 --> 00:13:55.123
The boat train to Paris is waiting.

00:13:55.131 --> 00:13:57.413
Now, tonight.

00:13:57.421 --> 00:14:00.439
Twenty to nine: we are seated.

00:14:00.447 --> 00:14:05.781
A quarter to nine: the whistle, and we are off, over Hungerford Bridge.

00:14:05.789 --> 00:14:09.176
Trains still leave Fogg's station this way.

00:14:09.184 --> 00:14:13.097
Our film is daylight; Fogg had rain and dark.

00:14:13.105 --> 00:14:18.636
Look right: the Embankment along the river, two years old in 1872.

00:14:18.644 --> 00:14:22.176
Beyond it, the clock tower, thirteen.

00:14:22.184 --> 00:14:25.281
New London slips away behind him.

00:14:25.289 --> 00:14:28.926
Today this train takes about two hours to Dover.

00:14:28.934 --> 00:14:32.676
Fogg's plan gave him seven days to reach Suez.

00:14:32.684 --> 00:14:34.860
This is the first hour.

00:14:34.868 --> 00:14:39.031
A few miles out, Passepartout sits up straight.

00:14:39.039 --> 00:14:44.749
He has forgotten to turn off the gas in his room. 'It will burn,' says Fogg, 'at your

00:14:44.749 --> 00:14:46.373
expense.'

00:14:46.381 --> 00:14:48.347
To be continued.

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:55.888
Your souvenir today: Fogg's last breakfast in London — the meal he ate before betting

00:14:55.888 --> 00:14:57.959
half his fortune.

00:14:57.969 --> 00:15:02.080
The dish is grilled fish with a Reading-sauce-style ketchup.

00:15:02.088 --> 00:15:08.475
Reading sauce was a real English sauce from 1802; it disappeared in the 1960s.

00:15:08.483 --> 00:15:11.067
The recipe is on the card.

00:15:11.075 --> 00:15:15.351
Cook the meal on the evening of the twenty-first of December, and sit down to eat by a

00:15:15.351 --> 00:15:17.304
quarter to nine.

00:15:17.312 --> 00:15:20.106
That is when Fogg promised to be back.

00:15:20.114 --> 00:15:25.133
His motto, and ours: 'A well-used minimum suffices for everything.'
